===============================================================================
TIER 2 QUARANTINE. READ THIS BEFORE COPYING ANYTHING OUT OF THIS DIRECTORY.
===============================================================================

Everything under this directory is Ubuntu security data (OVAL/USN, mirrored
here through Canonical's OSV-format export) and it carries a SHARE-ALIKE duty.

The operative obligation, from CC-BY-SA-4.0 section 3(b): where Adapted
Material is shared, "the Adapter's License You apply must be a Creative
Commons license with the same License Elements". A database that incorporates
this data and is then published can be an adaptation of it, and the same terms
would then attach to that database.

That is why this directory exists at all. Anvil's own findings database is a
Tier 0/1 publishable artifact, and a share-alike term reaching it is not a
warning, a caveat or a TODO: it is an obligation on everything Anvil ships,
and it cannot be withdrawn once published.

===============================================================================
WHAT IS ENFORCED IN CODE, AND WHAT IS NOT
===============================================================================

THIS SECTION IS ON ITS FIFTH REVISION AND THE FIRST FOUR WERE ALL FALSE. The
history is kept because it is the reason for the rule this revision follows.

  v1 presented the whole list as "enforced by internal/ingest/license (step
     A.4) and not merely written here". FALSE for the rule that matters most,
     the one about not publishing a merged corpus: no code in this repository
     can observe a publication.
  v2 claimed the gate refuses a tier 0/1 route for ANY source whose licence
     evidence carries a share-alike duty. FALSE. The detection was a table of
     substrings, and a substring table does not match licence prose: it was
     defeated by OSL-3.0's real operative wording, and then more damningly by
     mere FORMATTING — a hard line wrap, a non-breaking space, a doubled space,
     a full-width character. Every one of those is what a real licence file
     looks like.
  v3 replaced that with "reachable only by positive identification" and said
     "everything else is refused". FALSE, in a way the sentence hid rather than
     stated: the gate asked whether a permissive licence was PRESENT in the
     body, and a document can contain one while being something else entirely.
     A LICENSE reading "this tree is MIT; the vendored subtree under
     third_party/ is CDDL-1.0" contains exactly one enumerated permissive
     licence. Eight bodies of that shape published at tier 0 and tier 1 with
     the reciprocal terms attached.
  v4 fixed that, and then claimed at item 1(b) that admission requires that
     "NO OTHER LICENCE-LIKE CONTENT appears anywhere in the text", and at item
     3 that "every code point that RENDERS AS NOTHING is dropped". BOTH WERE
     FALSE, and both were disproved by bodies that publish today: an MIT
     LICENSE naming a subtree "under the GPL", a British-spelled "Eclipse
     Public Licence", an Apache-2.0 file bundling Apache-1.1, "under either the
     MIT License or the GPL", and a licence name split by a combining mark or
     an html tag.

THE RULE THIS REVISION FOLLOWS: A CLAIM THAT CANNOT BE DEMONSTRATED IS DELETED,
NOT QUALIFIED. Four rounds of more careful wording produced four false
documents; the pattern is the claiming, not the wording. Every item in the
first section below is a property that a NAMED TEST drives over its whole
domain. Everything that used to be in that section and is not there now has
moved to the second section, which is now the longer of the two. That is the
honest shape of this gate.

ENFORCED IN CODE by internal/ingest/license:

  1. ON A FRESH CLONE, NO FEED IS ADMITTED AT ALL — including this one.
     The gate refuses a feed outright unless the publisher's verbatim licence
     text has been acquired into this directory as LICENSE.full.txt and matches
     the sha256 pinned in mirror/LICENSE-MANIFEST.toml. No licence body is
     checked into this repository and EVERY sha256 in that manifest is empty,
     so no file can satisfy any pin. This is the item every other item rests
     on, and it is why the limits in the second section are not live today.
     Tests: TestFreshCloneAdmitsNoFeed, TestAnvilProseAloneCannotAdmitAnyFeed.

  2. NOTHING REACHES TIER 0 OR TIER 1 WITHOUT MATCHING AN ENUMERATED
     PERMISSIVE SIGNATURE. The publishable tiers are reachable only by a body
     that matches one of the licences ENUMERATED in
     internal/ingest/license/publishable.go, by a signature that is a
     contiguous phrase or a bounded window and that is not sitting inside an
     explicit denial within 96 bytes. Unrecognised, ambiguous and empty bodies
     are refused. UNKNOWN IS NOT PUBLISHABLE.
     This is the DEFAULT that matters: it does not depend on RECOGNISING a
     share-alike duty, so a reciprocal licence nobody anticipated is
     quarantined because it matched nothing, rather than by luck.
     WHAT IT IS NOT: proof that a matching body IS that licence. See item 8.
     Test: TestOnlyPositivelyIdentifiedPermissiveTextsReachThePublishableTiers,
     which drives bodies that are no enumerated licence at all — the operative
     clauses of OSL-3.0, EPL-2.0 3.2, CDDL-1.0 3.1 and MS-PL 3(D), each quoted
     so that it never names its own licence, plus an html-sourced, hard-wrapped,
     NBSP-laden CC-BY-SA deed. It asserts separately that the share-alike
     marker table does not classify four of those five, so it cannot be
     satisfied by adding a marker.

  3. A SHARE-ALIKE OR RESTRICTED DUTY THE MARKER TABLE RECOGNISES IS REFUSED A
     TIER 0/1 ROUTE, AND IS REFUSED FOR THAT REASON rather than as merely
     unrecognised. One it does NOT recognise is refused by item 2 instead,
     which is why item 2 is first. The obligation is read from the publisher's
     licence text, never from the feed table's label, and the STRONGEST match
     wins — so an Apache-2.0 identifier sitting over a GNU General Public
     License sentence classifies as share-alike.
     RECOGNISES IS THE OPERATIVE WORD: this is a table of substrings and it
     matches the wordings it lists and no others.
     Tests: TestSyntheticTier2RowCannotBeRoutedToTier0Or1,
     TestPermissiveTagOverShareAlikeBodyIsRefused,
     TestNONEDeclarationCannotHideAShareAlikeSource.

  4. MATCHING HAPPENS ONCE, AGAINST TEXT NORMALISED IN ONE PLACE. Whitespace
     runs (newlines and NBSP included) are collapsed to one space,
     compatibility forms are folded, case is folded, and every member of the
     shared invisible class is removed. Hard wrapping, an html &nbsp;, a
     doubled space and full-width characters no longer decide a licence
     conclusion.
     Test: TestNormalisationDefeatsTheFormattingEvasions, which asserts both
     that a plain substring match misses each marker and that the normalised
     match finds it.

     THE CLASS, PRECISELY, BECAUSE THIS ITEM USED TO OVERSTATE IT. It is not
     "every code point that renders as nothing" — nobody can enumerate that
     offline. It is: unicode.Cf, the TAG block, the zero-width and bidi block,
     the whole of Variation_Selector, the whole of
     Other_Default_Ignorable_Code_Point, and a declared eight-member supplement
     of blank glyphs that carry no property saying so. It has ONE owner,
     internal/ingest/invisible, shared with internal/ingest/sanitize.
     Test: TestBothConsumersDropEveryMemberOfTheClass, which sweeps the entire
     Unicode code space with no list on either side and no exclusions.
     THE TEST THIS ITEM USED TO CITE, TestBothConsumersAgree, IS NOW SKIPPED.
     It claimed the two consumers agree everywhere; they disagree on 959,049
     non-graphic code points, and the test excluded exactly that range in order
     to be green. It is kept, unexcluded and skipped, with the count in its
     skip message. A combining mark, an unassigned or private-use code point
     and an html tag all still split a marker — see item 8.

  5. license.CheckWritePath REFUSES any write path that does not belong to the
     tier that produced it, including Windows-separator and ../ forms. Data
     under mirror/tier2/ cannot reach mirror/tier0/ or mirror/tier1/ that way.
     Test: TestSyntheticTier2RowCannotBeRoutedToTier0Or1.

  6. This directory's tier and name are BOUND to the feed row through
     mirror/LICENSE-MANIFEST.toml, so a caller cannot point the gate at another
     source's licence file and inherit its conclusion.
     Test: TestPinIsBoundToTheFeedRow.

  7. EVERY REFUSAL CARRIES A TIER THAT IS NOT A VALID TIER (NoTier, -1), from
     license.Resolve as well as license.Gate, and a refused Decision projects
     onto NO license_dir_manifest row. A caller who ignores the error cannot
     read tier 0 — the most permissive tier there is — out of a refusal, and
     cannot write one into the A.2 cache's record of what may be merged.
     Tests: TestResolveRefusalNeverCarriesAPublishableTier,
     TestARefusalHasNoManifestRow.

NOT ENFORCED IN CODE. THE OPERATOR CARRIES THESE:

  8. THE ADMISSION PATH IS NOT TRUSTWORTHY. The refusal path is: nothing the
     gate refuses can be published, whatever the reason was. But an ADMISSION
     is a substring judgement wearing the word "identified", and there is no
     control here that establishes a body IS the licence it matched, or that it
     carries no second set of terms. EIGHT WORKING WAYS THROUGH IT ARE
     RECORDED, each with a body that publishes today, in the KNOWN LIMITS
     section at the top of internal/ingest/license/known_limits_test.go, and
     each is demonstrated by TestTheAdmissionPathIsNotTrustworthy:

       V1 ABBREVIATION. "the GPL", "the LGPL", "the AGPL" match no marker, and
          a bare "gpl" entry would fire on ordinary prose. An MIT LICENSE
          ending "The scripts in scripts/ are under the GPL." publishes.
       V2 BRITISH SPELLING. Every marker is spelled "license". "Eclipse Public
          Licence" and "Mozilla Public Licence 2.0" match nothing.
       V3 THE FAMILY EXEMPTION OVER-FIRES. A licence naming itself is exempted
          from the veto, and the families are coarser than the licences: an
          Apache-2.0 file that also bundles code under the Apache License,
          Version 1.1 — a different licence, with an advertising clause — is
          not vetoed. The same holds across the BSD family.
       V4 DUAL-LICENCE WORDING. "under either the MIT License or the GPL, at
          your choosing" and "the MIT License or, alternatively, the GPL
          version 3" match no scoping marker.
       V5 TITLE COLLISION. A signature is a phrase in the text, not a statement
          about the document. A commentary titled "Apache License, Version 2.0"
          whose body says "all rights are reserved" is identified as Apache-2.0.
       V6 LICENCE QUOTED IN PROSE. A CHANGELOG that quotes the MIT grant
          sentence is identified as MIT while the next sentence says the rest
          of the repository is proprietary.
       V7 NEGATION BEYOND THE 96-BYTE WINDOW. A document that denies the
          licence in its opening paragraph and states the grant 300 bytes later
          is admitted; the denial is out of range. Lengthening the window moves
          the hole rather than closing it.
       V8 CHARACTER SPLITTING, WHAT IS LEFT OF IT. The shared invisible class
          no longer splits a marker, but a COMBINING MARK ("Mozilla Pub" +
          U+0301 + "lic License"), an UNASSIGNED, PRIVATE-USE or NONCHARACTER
          code point (U+0378, U+E000, U+FDD0), and an HTML TAG ("Mozilla
          Pub<b></b>lic License") all still do.

     THAT LIST IS NOT A CENSUS. Three review rounds have run on this gate and
     each found new vectors after the previous round was called closed. Assume
     there are more. THE REAL FIX IS A LICENCE IDENTIFIER OVER A REAL CORPUS —
     a normalised full-text comparison against the SPDX licence list with a
     confidence score, the way askalono and licensee do it — and not more
     substrings; every vector above is a counter-example to a table, and
     patching each one individually creates the next.

     WHAT THIS MEANS FOR YOU. An operator who acquires licence bodies and
     switches feeds on MUST READ EACH ACQUIRED LICENCE TEXT. The gate is a
     second pair of eyes with the limits above, not a first pair. Item 1 is
     what makes this safe today, and item 1 stops being true the moment you
     fill in a digest.

  9. Do not publish a merged, normalised corpus that includes this data, and
     keep findings derived from it attributable to this tier. NO CODE IN THIS
     REPOSITORY CAN OBSERVE A PUBLICATION. The directory quarantine above is
     defence in depth against the mistake; it is not a guarantee against it,
     and no future version of A.4 can make it one.
 10. Attribution to Canonical Ltd. accompanies any redistribution of this
     data, alongside a link to the licence and an indication of changes made.
     Also unobservable from inside Anvil: no component here sees what you ship.
 11. This directory keeps its own licence file and is neither covered by nor a
     contributor to the repository's top-level LICENSE. A test asserts that the
     file exists and is non-empty. Nothing asserts that a downstream packager
     honoured the separation.

===============================================================================
PROVENANCE OF THIS LICENCE CONCLUSION
===============================================================================

Canonical's own security pages state no licence for this data. The
CC-BY-SA-4.0 assignment rests on the OSV project's source table
(research/01-vuln-data-sources-and-licensing.md S7), not on a statement by
Canonical (S29).

That is a weaker footing than a publisher's own words, and it is recorded
here rather than smoothed over. It points in the safe direction — quarantining
data that turns out to be permissive costs Anvil nothing, while publishing
data that turns out to be share-alike is unrecoverable — but it must be
re-verified against a Canonical legal page before Ubuntu is ingested directly
rather than through OSV.

===============================================================================
THIS FILE IS ANVIL'S RECORD. IT IS NOT THE PUBLISHER'S LICENCE TEXT.
===============================================================================

Everything above was written by Anvil. It records the identifier, the operative
obligation and the provenance of the conclusion, which is what a human needs in
order to act correctly — and it is deliberately NOT what the licence gate
admits a feed on. A document Anvil wrote in the same commit as the claim it
validates is not evidence of that claim.

The publisher's verbatim licence text lives beside this file as

    LICENSE.full.txt

which is NOT in git. mirror/LICENSE-MANIFEST.toml pins where it comes from and
what it must hash to; an operator acquires it deliberately with

    sh mirror/acquire-license-bodies.sh

and internal/ingest/license refuses this feed until that file is present and
matches its pin. The gate classifies THAT text. This file can only ever make
the gate stricter — if Anvil's record establishes a stronger duty than the
publisher's text does, the stronger one wins — and it can never admit a feed on
its own. See mirror/README.md.
